;;; A rule's false `(not (eq …))` conclusion is stored as a negation — M1f
;;; **W51**, register row `W51/not-eq-assert` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`),
;;; filed by S1f.5.7 (T1f.5.7.1, 2026-11-09) from its step 1's probes, `open`
;;; and owned by S1f.5.7, on the user's ruling of 2026-11-07 (Q-M1e2.26, G-21).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact `(p a)` and a rule concluding `(not (eq ?x ?x))` from `(p ?x)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `(not (eq x y))` is `(neq x y)` (Q-M1f.31, taken by default on the user's
;;; instruction of 2026-11-09), and a rule's conclusion of one is checked
;;; (G-21): at root `r` concludes `(neq a a)`, which is false, so root is a
;;; contradiction, explained as `(false)` is: its core is `(p a)` and
;;; `refuted by` names `r`. R is empty.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The conclusion is stored: root holds `(not (eq a a) :rule r)` (`ein
;;; saturate FILE --dump`), no contradiction pairs it, H is empty and R is
;;; {∅}, exit 0 (`ein solve FILE` and `ein oracle FILE` at `dc4c0bdc`, release
;;; and debug alike).

(relation p Node :why "{?1} is a node")
(p a)
(rule r ()
  :match (p ?x)
  :assert (not (eq ?x ?x))
  :why "{?x} is not itself")
